'' =================================================================================================
''
''   File....... test_bench_rc.spin2
''   Purpose.... The FlySky RC demo's control loop, unchanged, driven on the floor -- with a separate logger cog
''               streaming both wheels' state and the drive's events, so the log can show erratic handling
''   Authors.... Stephen M Moraco
''               -- Copyright (c) 2026 Iron Sheep Productions, LLC
''               -- see below for terms of use
''   E-mail..... stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... 27 Sep 2026
''   Updated.... 28 Sep 2026
''
''   Revision history:
''               27 Sep 2026 -- SRC_REV=1, FMT 1: first cut (tier floor-rc).
''               28 Sep 2026 -- SRC_REV=2, FMT 1: re-copied the demo's part after the demo's DEBUG-footprint fit
''                              (its lines built at run time and printed by one debug(), dbgShow()); every line
''                              prints as before.
''
'' =================================================================================================

{ WHAT THIS BINARY IS

  THE CONTROL LOOP IS THE DEMO'S, UNCHANGED IN BEHAVIOUR. Everything from the CON block titled timing through map() is
  copied from src/demo_dual_motor_rc.spin2 at its 28 Sep 2026 revision (HEAD e586ae9 plus the uncommitted batch, with
  the demo's DEBUG-footprint fit; its header reads "Updated.... 28 Sep 2026"; sha256 of the file copied from:
  2a20650fe60abe6084fabe6b3b6df1373d85d27f2e06506fe13e483fd85342d4): the same start (setStartChecks(true),
  waitUntilMotorReady()), the same stick mapping, the same swA / swB / swC / swD / swE / VRA handling, the same event
  and stop printing, the same doOneRotation(). To review, diff the two files. The ONLY differences in the demo's part:
   - lines marked "' RC:" in main() -- the banner, the logger's start and stop, RC-END, DEBUG_END_SESSION, and the
     cog-event quiet window (benchLog.cogEventBegin() / cogEventEnd(), PL-41 / PL-85) around each cog start and
     stop, which every bench program keeps;
   - one line marked "' RC:" in remoteControlMotor(), AFTER its loop: the quiet window before its wheels.stop();
   - the constant VALUE_NOT_SET is named RC_VALUE_NOT_SET here (same value, -2). This file includes
     isp_bldc_motor.spin2 for DRIVER_REV, whose own VALUE_NOT_SET (-1) the style gate would otherwise take this one
     for a copy of (rule 2.4); -1 is TRUE, which is exactly the value the demo's -2 exists to avoid;
   - the OBJ comment on the bench config names this tier, and a second OBJ block adds mtrConst and benchLog.
  Everything else this file adds sits after map(), under the CON block titled "the logger".

  HOW IT IS RUN (from the repo root)

      tools/bench-run.sh floor-rc        ->  pnut-ts -l -d -D BENCH_CFG -D BENCH_QUIET test_bench_rc.spin2

  pnut-term-ts, on the Raspberry Pi riding the platform, loads it over the PropPlug (P62/P63, 2 Mbaud) and logs every
  line. The run ends when the operator closes the terminal. swC DOWN ends the demo's loop ("* swC DN - end control"),
  and the program then reaches its natural end, prints DEBUG_END_SESSION and the terminal closes itself.

  WHY -D BENCH_QUIET. NOTHING MAY PRINT FROM THE STEERING FRONT COG OR THE DRIVERS: the front cog has a 1 ms slot with
  a ~517 us worst pass (PL-161), and one debug line costs hundreds of us. The drivers are PASM with no debug(). With
  the library's full channels the front cog prints its state report (showDriveStatesOnChange(), SENSE|FAULT) and its
  limit reports; the quiet masks (ERROR + WARNING, isp_bldc_motor_userconfig_bench.spin2) compile every one of them
  out -- the motion harness's rule I5 -- and no ERROR or WARNING statement sits in a front*() routine of either
  library object (read at DRIVER_REV 46). RC-BANNER prints quiet,TRUE|FALSE so a log says which build ran.
  Under BENCH_QUIET the demo's own lines and events print exactly as in the demo; the library's COMMAND / SENSE
  chatter on the main cog does not (swC middle prints "- drive states:", and showDriveStates() then prints nothing;
  RC-TEL carries those states).

  COG BUDGET, of 8: cog 0 main (the demo's control loop); the steering object's left driver, right driver and front
  cog (3); the S.BUS receiver (jm_sbus_rx's PASM cog, 1); the logger (1). 6 in use, 2 free. The DEBUG kernel takes no
  cog. RC-LOG prints free_cogs just after the logger starts, which should read 2.

  THE LOGGER COG (logger(), below). Started once the receiver runs, just before the demo's loop; stopped
  cooperatively (EXIT, a bounded wait for its acknowledgement, then cogstop) once the loop returns, so it is never
  stopped inside a debug() and can never leak the DEBUG lock (PL-41). It commands nothing, starts nothing and holds
  no lock but the DEBUG kernel's own. Every read is a hub read through a getter that holds no debug() at run time:
  the steering object's getPower(), getCurrent(), getHoldStatus(), testGetRawHallTicks(), testGetPathScale(),
  testLeftGetTelemetry() / testRightGetTelemetry(), testLeftGetDriveFollow() / testRightGetDriveFollow() and
  getEvent(); the receiver object's hasSignal(), readChannel(), isSwitchOn() / isSwitchOff() and chanMinMax() (its
  debug() lines are guarded by bShowDebug, FALSE unless showDebug(TRUE) is called, which nothing here does); and the
  demo's own priorSpeed / priorDirection.

  TORN READS -- the motion harness's rules (DOCs/plans/MOTION-HARNESS-DESIGN.md I3, I10), applied here:
   - every value is one aligned hub long (or, for a stick, one hub word the S.BUS cog writes with WRWORD): each read
     is atomic, so no single field can tear.
   - a wheel's fields come from a few grouped getter calls, not one atomic copy: testGet*Telemetry() reads its eight
     longs as one group (the motor object's own note: tighter than separate calls, still able to straddle one driver
     pass). A record is therefore a snapshot within tens of us, never a mix of samples further apart.
   - I3: a wheel's status is read as a measurement only while its driver runs (dcs <> DCS_Unknown; stop() writes
     DCS_Unknown after its cogstop). A wheel that is not running prints dcs 0 and every other field 0.
   - I10 in the one form this program allows: the logger never starts or stops a wheel; the demo's loop ends with
     wheels.stop() while the logger still runs, and a sample straddling that stop is gated by dcs on the next pass,
     as the harness's own stop exception is.
   - the event log (getEvent()) is built for many reading cogs: each cog has its own cursor, and a copy the front cog
     overwrote is detected by the log itself and reported as EV_LOST. The logger drains its OWN cursor, so the demo's
     event lines on cog 0 lose nothing to it.
   - the sticks: a word read cannot tear, but the channel set can straddle one S.BUS frame (the decode rewrites all
     16 channels in ~4 us, 130 frames a second). jm_sbus_rx's busy() and count() could bracket a whole frame, but
     isp_flysky_rx passes neither through; the demo reads the channels the same way.

  RATE (LOG_RATE_HZ = 25, one RC-TEL each 40 ms).
   - worst-case RC-TEL: 6 bytes of "CogN  " + 77 bytes of platform fields (tag, seq 9, ms 10, the stick and switch
     fields, path 5, late 5, 13 commas) + 2 x 72 bytes of wheel fields (pos 10, ci/fi 8 each, ma 7, duty 6, 13 commas
     each) + CR LF = 229 bytes; a typical record is about 150.
   - the link: 2 Mbaud 8N1 = 200_000 bytes/s. 25 x 229 = 5_725 bytes/s worst, 2.9 % of the link (typical 1.9 %).
     RC-EVT records are sporadic, at most LOG_EVENTS_PER_PASS (4) a pass, each under 90 bytes.
   - the logger: a record is on the wire 229 x 5 us = 1.15 ms worst; with its reads (about 20 getter calls), the
     line's formatting and a full event drain (4 x 0.45 ms) a pass is a few ms of its 40 ms period, so it cannot fall
     behind. RC-TEL's late field and RC-END's late / skipped / max_late_us / max_pass_us measure that on the floor.
   - why 25 and not 50: at 25 Hz one hall tick in a period is 25 ticks/s of tps; 50 Hz would double that coarseness
     for crawling speeds and double the stream (5.7 % of the link), for handling changes that take ~100 ms. RC-TEL
     carries each wheel's raw hall position (pos) too, so any rate can be re-derived over any window afterwards.

  RECORDS (FMT 1). One debug() line each, tag first, comma-separated, positional after RC-FIELDS names them.
   RC-BANNER  file,src_rev,fmt,drv_rev,cfg_id,clkfreq,quiet,mdbg,sdbg,log_hz,mirrors -- labelled pairs
   RC-FIELDS  the field names of RC-TEL, then of RC-EVT (printed once, before the logger starts)
   RC-ENUM    the dcs and hs values with their names, from the library's own constants
   RC-LOG     started,<TRUE|FALSE>,cog,<id>,hz,<rate>,free_cogs,<n>
   RC-TEL     seq,ms,sig,rt_vt,lt_hz,spd,dir,estop,sticks_off,cmd_spd,cmd_dir,path_pm,late, then per wheel, left
              (l_) then right (r_): pwr,ci_k,fi_k,pos,tps,ma,dcs,err,duty,short,fol_pct,hs,flt
   RC-EVT     seq,ms,wheel,kind,value -- wheel LEFT|RIGHT|PLATFORM, kind the EV_* name, value the SR_* name for
              EV_STOP, else the number
   RC-END     records,late,skipped,max_late_us,max_pass_us,acked -- labelled pairs, once, at the natural end
  seq numbers RC-TEL and RC-EVT together, 1, 2, 3 ...: a gap means a lost line. ms is getms(), the clock the event
  log stamps with. Numbers carry an underscore every three digits, as udec_() prints them. Flags print 0 / 1.
   sig        1 while the receiver hears the transmitter (hasSignal())
   rt_vt      right stick vertical, raw [0-2047] -- the demo's speed stick
   lt_hz      left stick horizontal, raw -- the demo's direction stick
   spd, dir   those two mapped exactly as the demo maps them, now [-100 to 100]
   estop      1 while swD is in cut-off (the demo's bEmerCutoff)
   sticks_off 1 while swA ignores the sticks (the demo's bIgnoreJoySticks)
   cmd_spd    the speed / direction the demo last sent to driveDirection() (its priorSpeed / priorDirection); the
   cmd_dir     demo reads the sticks once each 250 ms pass and sends only on a change
   path_pm    the steering path limiter's scale on both wheels, thousandths (1000 = unscaled)
   late       the logger's late passes so far (started more than 10 ms after due)
   pwr        the wheel's last commanded power, platform sense (getPower())
   ci_k       the increment last commanded, thousands; each MOTOR's own sense (the right wheel's is negated)
   fi_k       the field's increment per drive pass now, thousands, motor sense -- the ramp's actual output
   pos        the driver's raw hall-tick position, motor sense
   tps        hall ticks per second over this sample's period, from pos, motor sense
   ma         motor current, mA (getCurrent(), net of the rest zero)
   dcs        driver state, DCS_* (RC-ENUM); 0 = driver not running, every other field of the wheel then 0
   err        the driver's position error (lag), counts
   duty       the driver's duty
   short      1 while the lag limiter held the field within the last few slots (following fell short)
   fol_pct    the rpm window's measured rate as % of commanded; -1 while not valid (window filling, or stopped)
   hs         hold at rest, HS_* (RC-ENUM)
   flt        1 once the driver has latched a fault (isFaultSignal()'s latch)
}

CON { timing }

  CLK_FREQ = 270_000_000                                        ' system freq as a constant
  _clkfreq = CLK_FREQ                                           ' set system clock

CON { fixed io pins }

  RX1      = 63  { I }                                          ' programming / debug
  TX1      = 62  { O }

  SF_CS    = 61  { O }                                          ' serial flash
  SF_SCK   = 60  { O }
  SF_SDO   = 59  { O }
  SF_SDI   = 58  { I }

CON { application io pins }

    ' the S.BUS receiver's data line (README.md, "Wiring the FlySky Connection"). P58 is shared with the serial flash's
    '  data line (SF_SDI above), which the flash drives only while it is selected. Motor pins are set in
    '  isp_bldc_motor_userconfig.spin2.
    SBUS_RX  = 58  { I }

OBJ { our Drive Subsystem }

#ifdef BENCH_CFG
    user        : "isp_bldc_motor_userconfig_bench" ' bench rig configuration (-D BENCH_CFG, tools/bench-run.sh floor-rc)
#else
    user        : "isp_bldc_motor_userconfig"       ' driver configuration
#endif
    wheels      : "isp_steering_2wheel"             ' the dual-drive BLDC motors
    remoteCtl   : "isp_flysky_rx"                   ' the FlySky Transmitter
    names       : "demo_drive_names"                ' words for the drive's reported values, for the debug output

OBJ { RC: test_bench_rc's own }

    mtrConst    : "isp_bldc_motor"                  ' the constants only (DRIVER_REV): never started, never called
    benchLog    : "isp_bench_log"                   ' the cog-event quiet window, the free-cog count, the number format

VAR

    long    remoteCog


PUB main() | statusOK, basePinLt, basePinRt, voltage, motor, detectModeLt, detectModeRt, senseCog
'' DEMO Driving a two wheeled platform
''

' Local Variables:
' @local statusOK - TRUE when the S.BUS receiver cog started
' @local basePinLt - the validated left-motor base pin, or INVALID_PIN_BASE
' @local basePinRt - the validated right-motor base pin, or INVALID_PIN_BASE
' @local voltage - the validated drive voltage, or INVALID_VOLTAGE
' @local motor - the validated motor type, or INVALID_MOTOR
' @local detectModeLt - the validated left board detection mode, or INVALID_DET_MODE
' @local detectModeRt - the validated right board detection mode, or INVALID_DET_MODE
' @local senseCog - the steering front cog's id (0-7), or -1 when the motors did not start

    emitBanner()                                                ' RC: the build, before the demo's first line
    showLine(@"* demo dual motor control via R/C")

    ' validate user settings/choices
    '  do NOT start motor unless all are legit!
    basePinLt := wheels.validBasePinForChoice(user.LEFT_MOTOR_BASE)
    basePinRt := wheels.validBasePinForChoice(user.RIGHT_MOTOR_BASE)
    detectModeLt := wheels.validDetectModeForChoice(user.LEFT_BOARD_TYPE)
    detectModeRt := wheels.validDetectModeForChoice(user.RIGHT_BOARD_TYPE)
    voltage := wheels.validVoltageForChoice(user.DRIVE_VOLTAGE)
    motor := wheels.validMotorForChoice(user.MOTOR_TYPE)

    if basePinLt <> wheels.INVALID_PIN_BASE and basePinRt <> wheels.INVALID_PIN_BASE and voltage <> wheels.INVALID_VOLTAGE and motor <> wheels.INVALID_MOTOR and detectModeLt <> wheels.INVALID_DET_MODE and detectModeRt <> wheels.INVALID_DET_MODE
        ' 1. start() checks each wheel's hall sensors, current sense and motor leads with nothing moving, retries a
        '  check that fails, and refuses to start if one still fails. TRUE is the default; setStartChecks(false),
        '  called before start(), starts the platform anyway (getHealth() still reports what failed).
        wheels.setStartChecks(true)

        ' start our motor drivers (left and right)
        benchLog.cogEventBegin()                                ' RC: the cog-event quiet window (PL-41, PL-85)
        senseCog := wheels.start(basePinLt, basePinRt, voltage, detectModeLt, detectModeRt)
        benchLog.cogEventEnd()                                  ' RC: the new cogs' INIT lines are out
        if senseCog < 0
            ' start() returned -1: getError() says why, and for which wheel. A refused start check is
            '  ERR_START_CHECK_FAILED, and getHealth() then names the check -- a disconnected motor lead, say
            bCommandSucceeded(senseCog, @"start")
            showHealth()
        else
            showHealth()                                        ' what the start checks found, even when they passed
            showPack()
            showFaultResponse()

            ' override the default top speeds (75 %) with 100 %: the sticks reach the platform's full speed
            wheels.setMaxSpeed(100)
            wheels.setMaxSpeedForDistance(100)
            ' coast at rest: the default, set here so you can find it. holdAtStop(true) holds each wheel's position at
            '  rest instead, drawing current to do it
            wheels.holdAtStop(false)

            ' wait until both drivers are ready to be commanded, before anything moves
            waitUntilMotorReady()

            showLine(@"* Set up - remote control")

            benchLog.cogEventBegin()                            ' RC: the cog-event quiet window
            remoteCog := remoteCtl.start(SBUS_RX)
            benchLog.cogEventEnd()                              ' RC: the receiver cog's INIT line is out
            statusOK := remoteCog <> 0 ? TRUE : FALSE
            if statusOK
                ' if your transmitter's stick and knob ranges differ from isp_flysky_rx's defaults, calibrate them here:
                '   remoteCtl.manualCalibrate()
                showLine(@"R/C Running...")
                loggerStart()                                   ' RC: the telemetry stream, once the receiver runs
                remoteControlMotor()
                loggerStop()                                    ' RC: before the receiver and the drives stop
            else
                showLine(@"* ERROR S.BUS receiver did not start")

            ' turn off our SBUS drivers
            benchLog.cogEventBegin()                            ' RC: the cog-event quiet window
            remoteCtl.stop()
        ' turn off our motor drivers (safe whether or not they started)
        benchLog.cogEventBegin()                                ' RC: the cog-event quiet window
        wheels.stop()
    else
        showLine(@"* ERROR user configuration NOT valid!")

    showLine(@"* DONE")
    emitEnd()                                                   ' RC: the logger's totals
    debug("DEBUG_END_SESSION")                                  ' RC: QUOTED -- a backtick would make it a display command

PUB remoteControlMotor() | bIgnoreJoySticks, bDoneTesting, bShowState, bEmerCutoff, bShowDriveStates, accrate, bOneRotation, accRngMin, accRngMax
'' Listen to FlySky and control motor, get status based on Switch/Joystick inputs
''

' Local Variables:
' @local bIgnoreJoySticks - TRUE while switch A is ON: joystick driving is disabled
' @local bDoneTesting - TRUE when switch C is OFF: end the control loop
' @local bShowState - TRUE while switch B is OFF: show the motor currents
' @local bEmerCutoff - TRUE while switch D is OFF: emergency cutoff
' @local bShowDriveStates - TRUE while switch C is in its middle position: show the drive states
' @local accrate - the acceleration the VRA knob selects, mm/s squared
' @local bOneRotation - the channel-11 switch state: OFF asks for one rotation, ON re-arms it
' @local accRngMin - the VRA knob's lowest recorded reading
' @local accRngMax - the VRA knob's highest recorded reading

    waitms(500)    ' wait for 1/2 sec for backend to catch up

    showLine(@"------")
    showLine(@"- Lt Joy Hz - direction")
    showLine(@"- Rt Joy Vt - speed")
    showLine(@"------")
    showLine(@"* swA DN     - enable joystick driving")
    showLine(@"* swB DN     - show motor currents")
    showLine(@"* swC MID    - show drive states")
    showLine(@"* swC DN     - end control")
    showLine(@"* swD DN     - emer cutoff; back UP releases it and any protective stop")
    showLine(@"* swE DN     - 1 rotation")
    showLine(@"* VRA        - acceleration")
    showLine(@"------")

    bLastShowState := RC_VALUE_NOT_SET ' value can't happen
    bLastIgnoreJoyStks := RC_VALUE_NOT_SET ' value can't happen
    bLastEmerCutoff := RC_VALUE_NOT_SET ' value can't happen
    bLastShowDriveStates := RC_VALUE_NOT_SET ' value can't happen

    repeat
        ' SwA Ignore JoySticks
        bIgnoreJoySticks := remoteCtl.isSwitchOn(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_A)
        bShowState := remoteCtl.isSwitchOff(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_B)
        ' SwC done, end loop!
        bDoneTesting := remoteCtl.isSwitchOff(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_C)
        bShowDriveStates := remoteCtl.isSwitchMiddle(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_C)
        ' SwD down = Emergency Cutoff
        bEmerCutoff := remoteCtl.isSwitchOff(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_D)

        bOneRotation := remoteCtl.isSwitchOn(remoteCtl.CH_11)

        ' setAcceleration() takes mm/s squared, so the knob's travel is mapped onto a range in those units
        accRngMin, accRngMax := remoteCtl.chanMinMax(remoteCtl.CTL_VRA)
        accrate := map(remoteCtl.readchannel(remoteCtl.CTL_VRA), accRngMin, accRngMax, RC_ACCEL_MIN_MM_S2, RC_ACCEL_MAX_MM_S2)

        if bLastShowDriveStates <> bShowDriveStates
            if bShowDriveStates
                showLine(@"- drive states:")
                wheels.showDriveStates()
            bLastShowDriveStates := bShowDriveStates

        if bShowState <> bLastShowState
            if bShowState
                showCurrents()
            bLastShowState := bShowState

        if bEmerCutoff <> bLastEmerCutoff
            if bEmerCutoff
                showLine(@"-------  Motor E-OFF")
                wheels.emergencyCutoff()
            else
                ' 3. re-arming the kill switch is the operator's acknowledgement: it releases the emergency stop AND
                '  any protective stop (a blocked wheel), each of which latches until the program clears it. Both
                '  wheels stay stopped until the sticks command them.
                wheels.clearEmergency()
                wheels.clearProtectiveStop()
                if not bIgnoreJoySticks
                    showLine(@"  Motor Enabled")
                else
                    showLine(@"  Motor Disabled")
            bLastEmerCutoff := bEmerCutoff

        if bLastIgnoreJoyStks <> bIgnoreJoySticks
            if bIgnoreJoySticks
                showLine(@"  Motor CTL OFF")
            else
                showLine(@"  Motor CTL enabled")
            bLastIgnoreJoyStks := bIgnoreJoySticks

        if accrate <> 0 and nAccelerationRate <> accrate
            nAccelerationRate := accrate
            dbgBegin(@"  Setting Motor Acceleration:")
            dbgText(@"nAccelerationRate = ")
            dbgSdec(nAccelerationRate)
            dbgShow()
            wheels.setAcceleration(nAccelerationRate)

        if (not bEmerCutoff) and (remoteCtl.hasSignal() == FALSE)
            ' no transmitter -- the stick values are stale or the receiver's failsafe defaults, so
            '  nothing may drive on them: stop once, and drive again only when the signal returns
            if (priorSpeed <> 0) or (priorDirection <> 0)
                showLine(@"- SIGNAL LOST: stopping the motors")
                bCommandSucceeded(wheels.stopMotors(), @"signal lost stop")
                priorSpeed := priorDirection := 0
        elseif not bEmerCutoff
            rcSpeed, rcDirection := updPosnBothJoy()
            if not bIgnoreJoySticks
                if priorDirection <> rcDirection or priorSpeed <> rcSpeed
                    showJoy(@"- JOY")
                    bCommandSucceeded(wheels.driveDirection(rcSpeed, rcDirection), @"joystick drive")
                    priorDirection := rcDirection
                    priorSpeed := rcSpeed
            elseif priorSpeed <> 0 and rcSpeed == 0
                ' if we were running when motors control stopped then allow a stop when it happens
                showJoy(@"- JOY (last)")
                bCommandSucceeded(wheels.driveDirection(rcSpeed, rcDirection), @"joystick stop")
                priorDirection := rcDirection
                priorSpeed := rcSpeed
            elseif bOneRotation == false and nDoingOneRotation == false         ' no emergency cutoff and no joystick before accept 1 rotation
                nDoingOneRotation := true
                doOneRotation()
            elseif bOneRotation == true and nDoingOneRotation == true           ' switch must be reset before allowed again
                nDoingOneRotation := false

        showEvents()                                            ' 2. whatever the drive handled on its own since the last pass

        if bDoneTesting
            showLine(@"--> quit loop")
            quit

        waitms(250) ' wait 1/4 second, before next loop

    ' done, stop our motors
    benchLog.cogEventBegin()                                    ' RC: the cog-event quiet window (PL-41, PL-85)
    wheels.stop()

PRI bCommandSucceeded(eError, pWhat) : bSucceeded | eSteerError, eLeftError, eRightError
' Report a refused command with the cause and the wheel, and collect this cog's errors so the next failure records its own.
'
' @param eError - the result a command returned (a start()'s -1 is reported the same way)
' @param pWhat - pointer to a short name of what was attempted, for the report
' @returns bSucceeded - T/F where T means the command was accepted and its motion can be waited on

' Local Variables:
' @local eSteerError - this cog's first steering-object error (collected outside debug(), which a non-debug build removes)
' @local eLeftError - this cog's first left-wheel error
' @local eRightError - this cog's first right-wheel error

    bSucceeded := (eError == wheels.NO_ERROR)
    if bSucceeded == FALSE
        eSteerError, eLeftError, eRightError := wheels.getError()
        dbgBegin(@"* ERROR ")
        dbgText(pWhat)
        dbgText(@" refused: ")
        dbgText(names.errorName(eSteerError))
        dbgText(@", ")
        dbgWheels(names.errorName(eLeftError), names.errorName(eRightError))
        dbgShow()
        explainRefusal()

PRI explainRefusal() | eLeftCode, eRightCode
' After a refused drive: say what is holding the platform stopped, and what clears it.
'
' A drive is refused while a stop latches: an emergency stop, or a protective stop (a wheel commanded to move that
' could not -- blocked, say, against a kerb). getProtectiveStop() reads the latched cause without clearing it, and
' getStopReason() says how the last drive ended (SR_BLOCKED for the blocked wheel, SR_PARTNER for the other).

' Local Variables:
' @local eLeftCode - the left wheel's latched protective cause, NO_ERROR when none
' @local eRightCode - the right wheel's

    eLeftCode, eRightCode := wheels.getProtectiveStop()
    showStopReasons(@"  last stop")
    if (eLeftCode <> wheels.NO_ERROR) or (eRightCode <> wheels.NO_ERROR)
        dbgBegin(@"  PROTECTIVE STOP latched (")
        dbgWheels(names.errorName(eLeftCode), names.errorName(eRightCode))
        dbgText(@"): free the wheel, then flip swD to cut-off and back")
        dbgShow()

PRI showStopReasons(pLead) | eLeftReason, eRightReason
' Print why each wheel's last drive ended (getStopReason()), after a lead-in: "<lead>: LEFT <reason>, RIGHT <reason>".
' explainRefusal() and bWaitUntilMotorDone() both print this line.
'
' @param pLead - pointer to the text before ": LEFT"

' Local Variables:
' @local eLeftReason - the left wheel's SR_* value
' @local eRightReason - the right wheel's SR_* value

    eLeftReason, eRightReason := wheels.getStopReason()
    dbgBegin(pLead)
    dbgText(@": ")
    dbgWheels(names.stopReason(eLeftReason), names.stopReason(eRightReason))
    dbgShow()

PRI showHealth() | nLeftChecked, nLeftFailed, nLeftRecovered, nRightChecked, nRightFailed, nRightRecovered
' Report what start() checked on each wheel, which checks failed, and which failed at first and passed on a retry.
'
' getHealth() returns three masks of HLT_* bits per wheel. A recovered check started the motor normally -- worth
' knowing about (a loose connector can look like that), but not a failure.

' Local Variables:
' @local nLeftChecked - the left wheel's HLT_* bits that were checked
' @local nLeftFailed - ...that still failed
' @local nLeftRecovered - ...that failed, then passed on a retry
' @local nRightChecked - the right wheel's, likewise
' @local nRightFailed - the right wheel's still failing
' @local nRightRecovered - the right wheel's recovered

    nLeftChecked, nLeftFailed, nLeftRecovered, nRightChecked, nRightFailed, nRightRecovered := wheels.getHealth()
    dbgBegin(@"* start checks: LEFT checked ")
    dbgHex(nLeftChecked, 2)
    dbgText(@", RIGHT checked ")
    dbgHex(nRightChecked, 2)
    dbgShow()
    showHealthBits(@"LEFT", @"FAILED", nLeftFailed)
    showHealthBits(@"LEFT", @"recovered on a retry", nLeftRecovered)
    showHealthBits(@"RIGHT", @"FAILED", nRightFailed)
    showHealthBits(@"RIGHT", @"recovered on a retry", nRightRecovered)

PRI showHealthBits(pWheel, pWhat, nBits) | nBit
' Name each check set in a getHealth() mask.
'
' @param pWheel - pointer to the wheel's name
' @param pWhat - pointer to what the mask means (failed, or recovered)
' @param nBits - the mask of HLT_* bits

' Local Variables:
' @local nBit - the bit being tested

    repeat nBit from 0 to names.HEALTH_BITS - 1
        if nBits & (1 << nBit)
            dbgBegin(@"*   ")
            dbgText(pWheel)
            dbgText(@" ")
            dbgText(names.healthBit(nBit))
            dbgText(@" ")
            dbgText(pWhat)
            dbgShow()

PRI showPack() | ePackStatus, nMilliVolts
' Report the battery pack's voltage, when the optional pack voltage sensor is fitted (VOLTAGE-SENSOR.md).

' Local Variables:
' @local ePackStatus - PACK_NOT_FITTED, PACK_ABSENT or PACK_PRESENT
' @local nMilliVolts - the pack voltage, mV, when PACK_PRESENT

    ePackStatus, nMilliVolts := wheels.getPackVoltage()
    dbgBegin(@"* pack: ")
    dbgText(names.packStatus(ePackStatus))
    dbgText(@" ")
    dbgUdec(nMilliVolts)
    dbgText(@" mV")
    dbgShow()

PRI showFaultResponse() | eMode, brakePct
' Report the fault response in effect.
'
' FR_GRADED, the default, re-syncs from the hall sensors and stops along the ramp when a wheel's rotor cannot follow
' its field, which is gentle on a tall robot; if control is lost anyway it coasts, or with holdAtStop(TRUE) brakes at
' 10 %. FR_SHIPPED coasts or brakes with the full short at once (per holdAtStop()) instead:
'   wheels.setFaultResponse(wheels.FR_SHIPPED, 10)
' start() restores the default, so a program sets its choice after each start().

' Local Variables:
' @local eMode - FR_SHIPPED or FR_GRADED
' @local brakePct - the graded brake's share of a full short, %

    eMode, brakePct := wheels.getFaultResponse()
    dbgBegin(@"* fault response: ")
    dbgText(names.faultResponse(eMode))
    dbgText(@", brake ")
    dbgUdec(brakePct)
    dbgText(@" %")
    dbgShow()

PRI showEvents() | eKind, nMs, eWheel, nValue
' Drain this cog's event log: what the drive handled on its own, oldest first, both wheels merged.
'
' Each cog reads every event for itself. A cog that falls more than EV_QUEUE_DEPTH events behind is told first, with
' EV_LOST (its value: how many it lost) -- so a gap never reads as "nothing happened". EV_STOP carries the stop
' reason: a stop you did not command (SR_FAULT_*, SR_BLOCKED, SR_PARTNER) is how a driver learns the robot stopped
' itself. Most others carry a count or a measurement (DRIVE-OBJECTS.md, "Stop reasons and events").

' Local Variables:
' @local eKind - the event's EV_* kind; EV_NONE when all are read
' @local nMs - getms() when it happened
' @local eWheel - EVW_LEFT, EVW_RIGHT or EVW_PLATFORM
' @local nValue - the event's value

    repeat
        eKind, nMs, eWheel, nValue := wheels.getEvent()
        if eKind == wheels.EV_NONE
            quit
        dbgBegin(@"  event ")
        dbgUdec(nMs)
        dbgText(@" ms ")
        dbgText(wheelName(eWheel))
        dbgText(@" ")
        dbgText(names.eventKind(eKind))
        dbgText(@" ")
        if eKind == wheels.EV_STOP
            dbgText(names.stopReason(nValue))                  ' a stop's value is its reason, SR_*
        else
            dbgSdec(nValue)
        dbgShow()

PRI wheelName(eWheel) : pName
' Name getEvent()'s wheel: the left's, the right's, or the platform's own event (a late front-cog pass, the pack).
'
' @param eWheel - EVW_LEFT, EVW_RIGHT or EVW_PLATFORM
' @returns pName - pointer to its name

    case eWheel
        wheels.EVW_LEFT:
            pName := @"LEFT"
        wheels.EVW_RIGHT:
            pName := @"RIGHT"
        other:
            pName := @"PLATFORM"

PRI waitUntilMotorReady()
' if wheels are NOT ready, then wait until they are ready

    if wheels.isReady() == false
        showLine(@"* wait motors ready...")
        repeat
            if wheels.isReady()
                quit
            else
                waitms(2)
    showLine(@"* Motors ready, let's drive!")

PRI showCurrents()
' Print both wheels' motor current and power.

    ' getCurrent() reports each current in tenths of a mA (12_345 is 1.2345 A) and each power in mW
    nLtAmps, nLtWatts, nRtAmps, nRtWatts := wheels.getCurrent()
    dbgBegin(@"  current: LEFT ")
    dbgSdec(nLtAmps)
    dbgText(@" x0.1 mA ")
    dbgSdec(nLtWatts)
    dbgText(@" mW, RIGHT ")
    dbgSdec(nRtAmps)
    dbgText(@" x0.1 mA ")
    dbgSdec(nRtWatts)
    dbgText(@" mW")
    dbgShow()

PRI showJoy(pLead)
' Print the stick drive about to be sent, rcSpeed and rcDirection, after a lead-in.
'
' @param pLead - pointer to the text before the values

    dbgBegin(pLead)
    dbgText(@" rcSpeed = ")
    dbgSdec(rcSpeed)
    dbgText(@", rcDirection = ")
    dbgSdec(rcDirection)
    dbgShow()

PRI showLine(pText)
' Print one line of fixed text.
'
' @param pText - pointer to the zero-terminated text to print

    dbgBegin(pText)
    dbgShow()

CON { the debug line builder }

' WHY THIS DEMO BUILDS ITS DEBUG LINES. Each debug() statement is compiled into its own record, its text and its
' formatting included, and a -d image drops every record that lies too far into it (DOCs/procedures/
' PLOT-DISPLAY-RULES.md, rules 1-2). So this demo builds each line it prints in a hub buffer, with ordinary code that
' is in the image with or without -d, and prints it with the one debug() in dbgShow(). A line is started with
' dbgBegin(), extended with dbgText() / dbgUdec() / dbgSdec() / dbgHex() / dbgWheels(), and printed with dbgShow().
' The numbers come out as the debug formatters print them: dbgUdec() as udec_() and dbgSdec() as sdec_() (an
' underscore every three digits, 20_711), dbgHex() as uhex_byte_() or uhex_long_() ($0F, $09F4_9F49). A labelled
' formatter such as sdec(x) prints "x = 5", and ", " between two values in a row; those are written here as text.
' One cog -- the demo's main cog -- builds and prints lines.

    DBG_LINE_BYTES = 256                ' the longest line here is under 200 bytes; anything past the end is dropped

VAR

    byte    dbgLine[DBG_LINE_BYTES]     ' the line being built, zero-terminated
    long    dbgLineLen                  ' its length, not counting the terminator

PRI dbgBegin(pText)
' Start a new debug line with {pText}.
'
' @param pText - pointer to the line's first text

    dbgLineLen := 0
    dbgLine[0] := 0
    dbgText(pText)

PRI dbgText(pText)
' Append zero-terminated text to the debug line.
'
' @param pText - pointer to the text

    repeat strsize(pText)
        dbgChar(byte[pText++])

PRI dbgWheels(pLeft, pRight)
' Append the two wheels' words: "LEFT <left>, RIGHT <right>".
'
' @param pLeft - pointer to the left wheel's word
' @param pRight - pointer to the right wheel's word

    dbgText(@"LEFT ")
    dbgText(pLeft)
    dbgText(@", RIGHT ")
    dbgText(pRight)

PRI dbgUdec(nValue) | nPlace, nDigits
' Append {nValue} as an unsigned decimal, as udec_() prints it: an underscore every three digits (20_711).
'
' @param nValue - the value, taken as unsigned

' Local Variables:
' @local nPlace - the place value of the digit being appended (1, 10, 100 ...)
' @local nDigits - digits still to append

    nPlace := 1
    nDigits := 1
    repeat while (nPlace < 1_000_000_000) and ((nValue +/ nPlace) >= 10)    ' +/ and +// are the unsigned divides
        nPlace *= 10
        nDigits++
    repeat
        dbgChar("0" + ((nValue +/ nPlace) +// 10))
        nPlace /= 10
        nDigits--
        if (nDigits > 0) and (nDigits // 3 == 0)
            dbgChar("_")
    until nDigits == 0

PRI dbgSdec(nValue)
' Append {nValue} as a signed decimal, as sdec_() prints it (-15_090_904).
'
' @param nValue - the value

    if nValue < 0
        dbgChar("-")
        nValue := 0 - nValue            ' -2_147_483_648 stays $8000_0000, which dbgUdec() prints as 2_147_483_648
    dbgUdec(nValue)

PRI dbgHex(nValue, nDigits) | nNibble
' Append the low {nDigits} hex digits of {nValue} after a "$", with an underscore every four digits, as uhex_byte_()
' (2 digits, $0F) and uhex_long_() (8 digits, $09F4_9F49) print them.
'
' @param nValue - the value
' @param nDigits - how many hex digits to append

' Local Variables:
' @local nNibble - the digit's value, 0 to 15

    dbgChar("$")
    repeat while nDigits > 0
        nDigits--
        nNibble := (nValue >> (nDigits << 2)) & $F
        dbgChar(nNibble < 10 ? "0" + nNibble : "A" + nNibble - 10)
        if (nDigits > 0) and (nDigits // 4 == 0)
            dbgChar("_")

PRI dbgChar(charByte)
' Append one character to the debug line, dropping any past its end.
'
' @param charByte - the character

    if dbgLineLen < DBG_LINE_BYTES - 1
        dbgLine[dbgLineLen] := charByte
        dbgLineLen++
        dbgLine[dbgLineLen] := 0

PRI dbgShow()
' Print the debug line: this demo's one debug() statement (the section's header says why).

    debug(zstr_(@dbgLine))


PRI bWaitUntilMotorDone(maxMs) : bDone | startMs
' Wait, at most {maxMs}, for a limited drive to be over on both wheels -- however it ends -- then say why.
'
' Not used by the RC loop, which drives continuously; kept, bounded, for a program built from this one. isMoveDone() is
' TRUE on a fault or an emergency stop too, so this cannot hang on one (waiting for isStopped() alone did); a drive whose
' limit never fires is stopped at the bound.
'
' @param maxMs - the longest the drive may take, ms: its limit plus a margin
' @returns bDone - T/F where T means the drive ended by itself within {maxMs}

' Local Variables:
' @local startMs - getms() when the wait began

    startMs := getms()
    repeat until wheels.isMoveDone() or (getms() - startMs >= maxMs)
        waitms(MOVE_POLL_MS)
    bDone := wheels.isMoveDone()
    if bDone == FALSE
        wheels.stopMotors()                                     ' the drive outlived its bound: end it
    showStopReasons(bDone ? @"* Motors done" : @"* Motors TIMEOUT, stopped")   ' and why each wheel's drive ended

CON

   RC_VALUE_NOT_SET = -2                ' RC: the demo's VALUE_NOT_SET, renamed (the header says why); same value
   MOVE_POLL_MS  = 10                   ' bWaitUntilMotorDone()'s poll

   ' the VRA knob's travel maps onto this acceleration range, mm/s squared (setAcceleration())
   RC_ACCEL_MIN_MM_S2 = 200
   RC_ACCEL_MAX_MM_S2 = 3_000

VAR

    long    priorSpeed
    long    priorDirection
    long    rcSpeed
    long    rcDirection
    long    nLtAmps
    long    nLtWatts
    long    nRtAmps
    long    nRtWatts

    long    bLastShowState
    long    bLastEmerCutoff
    long    bLastIgnoreJoyStks
    long    bLastShowDriveStates
    long    nAccelerationRate
    long    nDoingOneRotation

PRI doOneRotation() | ltdegree, rtdegree, eError
' Drive forward slowly for one full wheel rotation, reporting the odometers it starts from.
'
' The rotation limit counts from its own call, not from the odometer (getRotationCount()), so it is 360 degrees
' whatever the odometer reads. This returns at once, so the control loop keeps reading the kill switch; the end of the
' rotation arrives as an EV_STOP event (SR_AT_LIMIT), which the loop's showEvents() prints.

' Local Variables:
' @local ltdegree - the left wheel's odometer, degrees
' @local rtdegree - the right wheel's odometer, degrees
' @local eError - the result of the rotation limit, then of the drive it guards

    ltdegree, rtdegree := wheels.getRotationCount(wheels.DRU_DEGREES)           ' the odometers before the rotation
    ' arm the limit first and drive only if it was accepted, so the wheels never run without one
    eError := wheels.stopAfterRotation(360, wheels.DRU_DEGREES)                 ' stop after one wheel rotation
    if eError == wheels.NO_ERROR
        eError := wheels.driveDirection(10, 0)                                  ' drive forward slowly
    if bCommandSucceeded(eError, @"one rotation")
        dbgBegin(@"  one rotation: driving, odometers from")
        dbgText(@"ltdegree = ")
        dbgSdec(ltdegree)
        dbgText(@", rtdegree = ")
        dbgSdec(rtdegree)
        dbgText(@" degrees")
        dbgShow()

PRI updPosnBothJoy(): speed, direction | rawSpeed, rawDirection, rngMin, rngMax
' Read both joysticks and scale them to a drive speed and direction.
'
' @returns speed - the right stick's vertical position as a speed [-100 to 100]
' @returns direction - the left stick's horizontal position as a direction [-100 to 100]

' Local Variables:
' @local rawSpeed - the right stick's vertical channel reading
' @local rawDirection - the left stick's horizontal channel reading
' @local rngMin - the channel's lowest recorded reading
' @local rngMax - the channel's highest recorded reading

    ' route Rt Joystick Vt (spring center) to FWD/REV (speed)
    'debug("-- JOY -- JOY --")
    rawSpeed := remoteCtl.readChannel(remoteCtl.CTL_RT_JOY_VT)
    rngMin, rngMax := remoteCtl.chanMinMax(remoteCtl.CTL_RT_JOY_VT)
    speed := map(rawSpeed, rngMin, rngMax, -100, +100)

    ' route Lt Joystick Hz (spring center) to direction
    rawDirection := remoteCtl.readChannel(remoteCtl.CTL_LT_JOY_HZ)
    rngMin, rngMax := remoteCtl.chanMinMax(remoteCtl.CTL_LT_JOY_HZ)
    direction := map(rawDirection, rngMin, rngMax, -100, +100)

PRI map(inValue,  inMin,  inMax,  outMin,  outMax) : outValue
' Re-map a value from one range onto another, after Arduino's map()
'  (REF: https://www.arduino.cc/reference/en/language/functions/math/map), with two differences:
'  - it clamps: an input at or beyond an end of [inMin, inMax] returns that end of the output range
'  - so {inMin} must be below {inMax}; the output range may run either way (outMin > outMax reverses it)
'  Integer math: fractions are truncated, not rounded.
'
' @param inValue - the value to re-map
' @param inMin - the input range's low end
' @param inMax - the input range's high end
' @param outMin - the output value for {inMin}
' @param outMax - the output value for {inMax}
' @returns outValue - {inValue} re-mapped onto [outMin, outMax], clamped to its ends

    if (inValue <= inMin)
        outValue := outMin
    elseif (inValue >= inMax)
        outValue :=  outMax
    else
        outValue := (inValue - inMin) * (outMax - outMin) / (inMax - inMin) + outMin

CON { --- the logger: everything below is test_bench_rc's own; nothing above calls it but the RC: lines --- }

CON { build identity }

    SRC_REV     = 2                                             ' hand-bumped whenever this file changes; printed in RC-BANNER
    FMT_VERSION = 1                                             ' RC- record-format version: bumped when a record changes shape

CON { logger cog }

    LOG_RATE_HZ         = 25                                    ' one RC-TEL each 40 ms: the header's RATE section
    LOG_PERIOD_TICKS    = CLK_FREQ / LOG_RATE_HZ                ' 10_800_000 at 270 MHz
    LOG_LATE_TICKS      = LOG_PERIOD_TICKS / 4                  ' a pass later than this (10 ms) is counted late
    TICKS_PER_US        = CLK_FREQ / 1_000_000
    ' the smallest slack the logger will waitct() on: below it the target could pass before waitct() runs, which would
    '  wait for the counter to wrap (~15.9 s at 270 MHz) -- the instrument cog's rule (test_bench_dual.spin2)
    LOG_MIN_SLACK_TICKS = 200 * TICKS_PER_US
    LOG_EVENTS_PER_PASS = 4                                     ' RC-EVT per pass at most: 100 a second, far above the log's rate
    LOG_STACK_LONGS     = 256                                   ' the instrument's 128, doubled; its scratch lives in VAR too
    LOG_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS  = 250                                   ' one period plus a pass, with margin
    LOG_POLL_MS         = 1
    LOG_PARK_MS         = 100
    INCR_PER_K          = 1_000                                 ' ci_k and fi_k are increments / 1_000
    TENTHS_MA_PER_MA    = 10                                    ' getCurrent() reports 0.1 mA
    FOLLOW_NOT_VALID    = -1                                    ' fol_pct while the rpm window is not valid
    ' the RC-TEL line: 221 bytes worst (the header's RATE section, less "CogN  " and CR LF); anything past the buffer
    '  is dropped, so a line can come out short but never overrun. Numbers are clamped to benchLog.LIM_BIG, 12 bytes.
    LINE_BUF_BYTES      = 256
    ' the quiet debug masks (isp_bldc_motor_userconfig_bench.spin2 under -D BENCH_QUIET)
    QUIET_DBG_MASK      = DECOD user.DBGCH_ERROR | DECOD user.DBGCH_WARNING

  ' logger commands (cog 0 writes):
  '  LOG_CMD_RUN  - sample and print
  '  LOG_CMD_EXIT - finish this pass, acknowledge, park
    #0, LOG_CMD_RUN, LOG_CMD_EXIT

  ' logger states (the logger writes):
  '  LOG_ST_NONE    - not started, or not yet in its loop
  '  LOG_ST_RUNNING - sampling and printing
  '  LOG_ST_EXITED  - parked, printing nothing; safe to cogstop
    #0, LOG_ST_NONE, LOG_ST_RUNNING, LOG_ST_EXITED

  ' the logger's per-wheel slots
    #0, SLOT_LEFT, SLOT_RIGHT
    LOG_SLOTS = 2

VAR { logger shared memory -- one writer each }

    ' written by cog 0, read by the logger
    LONG    logCmd
    ' written by the logger, read by cog 0 (the totals only once the logger has acknowledged EXIT or been stopped)
    LONG    logState
    LONG    logSeq                                          ' records emitted, RC-TEL and RC-EVT together
    LONG    logLate                                         ' passes started more than LOG_LATE_TICKS after their due time
    LONG    logSkipped                                      ' whole periods that got no sample
    LONG    logMaxLateTicks
    LONG    logMaxPassTicks                                 ' the longest pass: its reads, its RC-TEL and its RC-EVTs
    ' owned by cog 0
    LONG    logCogId                                        ' cogspin()'s return; < 0 when it failed or was never tried
    LONG    bLogAcked                                       ' the logger acknowledged EXIT before its cogstop
    LONG    logStack[LOG_STACK_LONGS]

VAR { logger scratch -- written and read by the logger cog only (keeps its stack small) }

    LONG    lgMs                                            ' getms() at the sample
    LONG    lgSig
    LONG    lgRawVt
    LONG    lgRawHz
    LONG    lgSpd
    LONG    lgDir
    LONG    lgEstop
    LONG    lgIgnore
    LONG    lgCmdSpd
    LONG    lgCmdDir
    LONG    lgPath
    LONG    lgLastCt                                        ' getct() at the previous sample
    LONG    lgPwr[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgCi[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgFi[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgPos[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgLastPos[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgTps[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgMa[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgDcs[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgErr[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgDuty[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgShort[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgFol[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgHs[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgFlt[LOG_SLOTS]
    LONG    lgLineLen                                       ' bytes in lgLine, excluding its terminator
    BYTE    lgLine[LINE_BUF_BYTES]                          ' the RC-TEL line under assembly

DAT { record text -- hub strings, emitted with zstr_() (DBG-2) }

sFieldsTel      BYTE    "RC-FIELDS,RC-TEL,seq,ms,sig,rt_vt,lt_hz,spd,dir,estop,sticks_off,cmd_spd,cmd_dir,path_pm,late,"
                BYTE    "l_pwr,l_ci_k,l_fi_k,l_pos,l_tps,l_ma,l_dcs,l_err,l_duty,l_short,l_fol_pct,l_hs,l_flt,"
                BYTE    "r_pwr,r_ci_k,r_fi_k,r_pos,r_tps,r_ma,r_dcs,r_err,r_duty,r_short,r_fol_pct,r_hs,r_flt", 0
sFieldsEvt      BYTE    "RC-FIELDS,RC-EVT,seq,ms,wheel,kind,value", 0
#ifdef BENCH_CFG
sCfgId          BYTE    "BENCH", 0
#else
sCfgId          BYTE    "USER", 0
#endif
sTrue           BYTE    "TRUE", 0
sFalse          BYTE    "FALSE", 0

PRI emitBanner() | bQuiet
' RC-BANNER: the build -- this file's revision and record format, the library's DRIVER_REV, the config, the clock, and
' whether the quiet debug masks are compiled (the front cog then holds no debug()). Worst case about 200 bytes.

' Local Variables:
' @local bQuiet - TRUE when both library masks are the quiet set (ERROR + WARNING)

    bQuiet := (user.MOTOR_DBG_MASK == QUIET_DBG_MASK) and (user.STEER_DBG_MASK == QUIET_DBG_MASK)
    debug("RC-BANNER,file,test_bench_rc,src_rev,", udec_(SRC_REV), ",fmt,", udec_(FMT_VERSION), ",drv_rev,", udec_(mtrConst.DRIVER_REV), ",cfg_id,", zstr_(@sCfgId), ",clkfreq,", udec_(clkfreq), ",quiet,", zstr_(boolToken(bQuiet)), ",mdbg,", udec_(user.MOTOR_DBG_MASK), ",sdbg,", udec_(user.STEER_DBG_MASK), ",log_hz,", udec_(LOG_RATE_HZ), ",mirrors,demo_dual_motor_rc")

PRI loggerStart()
' Name the stream's fields, then start the logger cog inside the cog-event quiet window, and report it (RC-LOG).
' A logger that does not start leaves the demo running without telemetry: RC-LOG says so and RC-END reads 0 records.

    logCmd := LOG_CMD_RUN
    logState := LOG_ST_NONE
    logSeq := 0
    logLate := 0
    logSkipped := 0
    logMaxLateTicks := 0
    logMaxPassTicks := 0
    bLogAcked := FALSE
    debug(zstr_(@sFieldsTel))
    debug(zstr_(@sFieldsEvt))
    debug("RC-ENUM,dcs,", udec_(wheels.DCS_Unknown), ",DCS_Unknown,", udec_(wheels.DCS_STOPPED), ",DCS_STOPPED,", udec_(wheels.DCS_SPIN_UP), ",DCS_SPIN_UP,", udec_(wheels.DCS_AT_SPEED), ",DCS_AT_SPEED,", udec_(wheels.DCS_SPIN_DN), ",DCS_SPIN_DN,", udec_(wheels.DCS_SLOW_TO_CHG), ",DCS_SLOW_TO_CHG,", udec_(wheels.DCS_FAULTED), ",DCS_FAULTED,", udec_(wheels.DCS_ESTOP), ",DCS_ESTOP,", udec_(wheels.DCS_RELEASED), ",DCS_RELEASED")
    debug("RC-ENUM,hs,", udec_(wheels.HS_OFF), ",HS_OFF,", udec_(wheels.HS_HOLDING), ",HS_HOLDING,", udec_(wheels.HS_SLIPPED), ",HS_SLIPPED,", udec_(wheels.HS_LIMITED), ",HS_LIMITED")
    benchLog.cogEventBegin()
    logCogId := cogspin(NEWCOG, logger(), @logStack)
    benchLog.cogEventEnd()
    debug("RC-LOG,started,", zstr_(boolToken(logCogId >= 0)), ",cog,", sdec_(logCogId), ",hz,", udec_(LOG_RATE_HZ), ",free_cogs,", udec_(benchLog.countFreeCogs()))

PRI loggerStop() | startMs
' Stop the logger cooperatively: command EXIT, wait (bounded) for it to acknowledge from its park, then cogstop it.
' It checks the command only between passes, so an acknowledged logger is never inside a debug() (PL-41).

' Local Variables:
' @local startMs - getms() when EXIT was commanded

    if logCogId >= 0
        logCmd := LOG_CMD_EXIT
        startMs := getms()
        repeat until (logState == LOG_ST_EXITED) or (getms() - startMs >= LOG_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS)
            waitms(LOG_POLL_MS)
        bLogAcked := (logState == LOG_ST_EXITED)
        benchLog.cogEventBegin()
        cogstop(logCogId)

PRI emitEnd()
' RC-END: the logger's totals, once, at the program's natural end -- how many records it emitted (the last seq), and
' whether it ever fell behind. All 0 when it never started.

    debug("RC-END,records,", udec_(logSeq), ",late,", udec_(logLate), ",skipped,", udec_(logSkipped), ",max_late_us,", udec_(logMaxLateTicks / TICKS_PER_US), ",max_pass_us,", udec_(logMaxPassTicks / TICKS_PER_US), ",acked,", zstr_(boolToken(bLogAcked)))

PRI boolToken(bFlag) : pToken
' Name a boolean for a labelled record field: TRUE or FALSE, never a number (the bench's decision 13).
'
' @param bFlag - the flag; any non-zero is TRUE
' @returns pToken - pointer to "TRUE" or "FALSE"

    pToken := (bFlag <> FALSE) ? @sTrue : @sFalse

PRI flagDigit(bFlag) : nDigit
' A flag as one digit for RC-TEL's positional fields: 1 or 0 (compact; a Spin2 TRUE would print as -1).
'
' @param bFlag - the flag; any non-zero counts as set
' @returns nDigit - 1 when set, else 0

    nDigit := 0
    if bFlag <> FALSE
        nDigit := 1

CON { --- the logger cog: runs in its own cog; it prints, and nothing else it does can block the drive --- }

PRI logger() | dueCt, slackTicks, passCt
' THE LOGGER COG (loggerStart()'s cogspin). Paces itself on an absolute getct() schedule, LOG_RATE_HZ, never calling
' waitct() without positive slack; each pass reads the sticks and both wheels, prints one RC-TEL, then drains up to
' LOG_EVENTS_PER_PASS of its own events as RC-EVT. A late pass is counted and the schedule re-based, never replayed.
' Reads only: commands nothing, starts and stops nothing. Checks the EXIT command between passes, acknowledges it,
' and parks until loggerStop() cogstops it; it never returns.
'
' @local dueCt - getct() the next sample is due at
' @local slackTicks - dueCt - getct(), signed (a GETCT difference is wrap-safe)
' @local passCt - getct() when this pass's work began

    lgLastCt := getct()
    lgLastPos[SLOT_LEFT], lgLastPos[SLOT_RIGHT] := wheels.testGetRawHallTicks()
    logState := LOG_ST_RUNNING
    dueCt := getct() + LOG_PERIOD_TICKS
    repeat while logCmd <> LOG_CMD_EXIT
        slackTicks := dueCt - getct()
        if slackTicks > LOG_MIN_SLACK_TICKS
            waitct(dueCt)
            dueCt += LOG_PERIOD_TICKS
        elseif slackTicks > 0
            dueCt += LOG_PERIOD_TICKS                           ' on time, too close to the target to wait on
        else
            logCountLate(0 - slackTicks)
            if (0 - slackTicks) >= LOG_PERIOD_TICKS
                dueCt := getct() + LOG_PERIOD_TICKS             ' re-base; the skipped periods were counted
            else
                dueCt += LOG_PERIOD_TICKS
        passCt := getct()
        logSample()
        logEvents()
        logMaxPassTicks #>= getct() - passCt
    logState := LOG_ST_EXITED
    repeat
        waitms(LOG_PARK_MS)

PRI logCountLate(lateTicks)
' Count one pass that started after its due time (logger cog): late only past LOG_LATE_TICKS, the largest lateness,
' and the whole periods that got no sample.
'
' @param lateTicks - how far past its due time the pass starts, in ticks (> 0)

    if lateTicks > LOG_LATE_TICKS
        logLate++
    logMaxLateTicks #>= lateTicks
    if lateTicks >= LOG_PERIOD_TICKS
        logSkipped += lateTicks / LOG_PERIOD_TICKS

PRI logSample() | nowCt, periodTicks, rngMin, rngMax, slotIdx, nDelta, nFollowPct, bFollowOk, bShort, bFault, nTenthsL, nTenthsR
' Read one sample -- the sticks and switches the demo acts on, then both wheels -- and print it as one RC-TEL (logger
' cog). Each wheel's telemetry group is read first: its dcs is the gate (I3) for the rest of that wheel's fields.
'
' @local nowCt - getct() at the sample, the rate's timebase
' @local periodTicks - ticks since the previous sample
' @local rngMin - a stick channel's lowest recorded reading
' @local rngMax - a stick channel's highest recorded reading
' @local slotIdx - wheel slot
' @local nDelta - hall ticks since the previous sample
' @local nFollowPct - the rpm window's following percentage, as read
' @local bFollowOk - T/F where T means that percentage is valid
' @local bShort - T/F where T means the lag limiter held the field lately
' @local bFault - T/F where T means the driver's fault latch is set
' @local nTenthsL - the left motor's current, 0.1 mA
' @local nTenthsR - the right motor's current, 0.1 mA

    lgMs := getms()
    nowCt := getct()

    ' 1. the sticks and switches, read and mapped as the demo reads and maps them
    lgSig := flagDigit(remoteCtl.hasSignal())
    lgRawVt := remoteCtl.readChannel(remoteCtl.CTL_RT_JOY_VT)
    rngMin, rngMax := remoteCtl.chanMinMax(remoteCtl.CTL_RT_JOY_VT)
    lgSpd := map(lgRawVt, rngMin, rngMax, -100, +100)
    lgRawHz := remoteCtl.readChannel(remoteCtl.CTL_LT_JOY_HZ)
    rngMin, rngMax := remoteCtl.chanMinMax(remoteCtl.CTL_LT_JOY_HZ)
    lgDir := map(lgRawHz, rngMin, rngMax, -100, +100)
    lgEstop := flagDigit(remoteCtl.isSwitchOff(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_D))
    lgIgnore := flagDigit(remoteCtl.isSwitchOn(remoteCtl.CTL_SW_A))
    lgCmdSpd := priorSpeed                                      ' the demo's last command, one long each
    lgCmdDir := priorDirection

    ' 2. each wheel: the telemetry group first (it carries the gate), then the rest
    lgDuty[SLOT_LEFT], lgErr[SLOT_LEFT], _, _, _, _, lgDcs[SLOT_LEFT], bFault := wheels.testLeftGetTelemetry()
    lgFlt[SLOT_LEFT] := flagDigit(bFault)
    lgDuty[SLOT_RIGHT], lgErr[SLOT_RIGHT], _, _, _, _, lgDcs[SLOT_RIGHT], bFault := wheels.testRightGetTelemetry()
    lgFlt[SLOT_RIGHT] := flagDigit(bFault)
    lgCi[SLOT_LEFT], lgFi[SLOT_LEFT], bShort, _, nFollowPct, bFollowOk := wheels.testLeftGetDriveFollow()
    lgShort[SLOT_LEFT] := flagDigit(bShort)
    lgFol[SLOT_LEFT] := (bFollowOk <> FALSE) ? nFollowPct : FOLLOW_NOT_VALID
    lgCi[SLOT_RIGHT], lgFi[SLOT_RIGHT], bShort, _, nFollowPct, bFollowOk := wheels.testRightGetDriveFollow()
    lgShort[SLOT_RIGHT] := flagDigit(bShort)
    lgFol[SLOT_RIGHT] := (bFollowOk <> FALSE) ? nFollowPct : FOLLOW_NOT_VALID
    lgPwr[SLOT_LEFT], lgPwr[SLOT_RIGHT] := wheels.getPower()
    lgPos[SLOT_LEFT], lgPos[SLOT_RIGHT] := wheels.testGetRawHallTicks()
    nTenthsL, _, nTenthsR, _ := wheels.getCurrent()
    lgMa[SLOT_LEFT] := nTenthsL / TENTHS_MA_PER_MA
    lgMa[SLOT_RIGHT] := nTenthsR / TENTHS_MA_PER_MA
    lgHs[SLOT_LEFT], _, lgHs[SLOT_RIGHT], _ := wheels.getHoldStatus()
    lgPath := wheels.testGetPathScale()

    ' 3. the rates, and the I3 gate: a wheel whose driver is not running reports dcs 0 and nothing else
    periodTicks := nowCt - lgLastCt
    lgLastCt := nowCt
    repeat slotIdx from SLOT_LEFT to SLOT_RIGHT
        nDelta := lgPos[slotIdx] - lgLastPos[slotIdx]
        lgLastPos[slotIdx] := lgPos[slotIdx]
        lgTps[slotIdx] := 0
        if periodTicks > 0
            lgTps[slotIdx] := muldiv64(abs(nDelta), CLK_FREQ, periodTicks)     ' magnitude first, then the sign
            if nDelta < 0
                lgTps[slotIdx] := 0 - lgTps[slotIdx]
        lgCi[slotIdx] /= INCR_PER_K
        lgFi[slotIdx] /= INCR_PER_K
        if lgDcs[slotIdx] == wheels.DCS_Unknown
            lgPwr[slotIdx] := lgCi[slotIdx] := lgFi[slotIdx] := lgPos[slotIdx] := lgTps[slotIdx] := 0
            lgMa[slotIdx] := lgErr[slotIdx] := lgDuty[slotIdx] := lgShort[slotIdx] := lgFol[slotIdx] := 0
            lgHs[slotIdx] := lgFlt[slotIdx] := 0

    ' 4. one line, built here and sent with ONE zstr_(): a debug() takes at most 31 expressions and RC-TEL carries 39
    logSeq++
    lineBegin(@"RC-TEL")
    lineNum(logSeq)
    lineNum(lgMs)
    lineNum(lgSig)
    lineNum(lgRawVt)
    lineNum(lgRawHz)
    lineNum(lgSpd)
    lineNum(lgDir)
    lineNum(lgEstop)
    lineNum(lgIgnore)
    lineNum(lgCmdSpd)
    lineNum(lgCmdDir)
    lineNum(lgPath)
    lineNum(logLate)
    repeat slotIdx from SLOT_LEFT to SLOT_RIGHT
        lineNum(lgPwr[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgCi[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgFi[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgPos[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgTps[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgMa[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgDcs[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgErr[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgDuty[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgShort[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgFol[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgHs[slotIdx])
        lineNum(lgFlt[slotIdx])
    debug(zstr_(@lgLine))

PRI logEvents() | eKind, nMs, eWheel, nValue, nRead
' Drain the LOGGER COG's own event cursor, at most LOG_EVENTS_PER_PASS a pass, one RC-EVT each (logger cog). The log
' keeps a cursor per reading cog, so this takes nothing from the demo's showEvents() on cog 0; a lapped cursor reads
' EV_LOST first, with how many were lost.
'
' @local eKind - the event's EV_* kind; EV_NONE when all are read
' @local nMs - getms() when it happened
' @local eWheel - EVW_LEFT, EVW_RIGHT or EVW_PLATFORM
' @local nValue - the event's value (the SR_* reason for EV_STOP)
' @local nRead - events read this pass

    nRead := 0
    repeat while nRead < LOG_EVENTS_PER_PASS
        eKind, nMs, eWheel, nValue := wheels.getEvent()
        if eKind == wheels.EV_NONE
            quit
        nRead++
        logSeq++
        if eKind == wheels.EV_STOP
            debug("RC-EVT,", udec_(logSeq), ",", udec_(nMs), ",", zstr_(wheelName(eWheel)), ",", zstr_(names.eventKind(eKind)), ",", zstr_(names.stopReason(nValue)))
        else
            debug("RC-EVT,", udec_(logSeq), ",", udec_(nMs), ",", zstr_(wheelName(eWheel)), ",", zstr_(names.eventKind(eKind)), ",", sdec_(nValue))

PRI lineBegin(pTag) | srcIdx
' Start the RC-TEL line with its tag (logger cog).
'
' @param pTag - pointer to the record's tag text
'
' @local srcIdx - index into the tag

    lgLineLen := 0
    lgLine[0] := 0
    repeat srcIdx from 0 to LINE_BUF_BYTES - 1
        if byte[pTag][srcIdx] == 0
            quit
        lineChar(byte[pTag][srcIdx])

PRI lineNum(numValue) | digitStack[benchLog.MAX_DEC_DIGITS], digitCount, absMagnitude, digitIdx
' Append "," and a signed decimal with an underscore every three digits -- the form udec_() / sdec_() print and
' isp_bench_log.spin2 builds -- clamped to +/- benchLog.LIM_BIG so no field runs wider than 12 bytes (logger cog).
'
' @param numValue - the field's value
'
' @local digitStack - decimal digits of the clamped value, least significant first
' @local digitCount - how many entries of digitStack are in use
' @local absMagnitude - magnitude of the clamped value, consumed by the extraction loop
' @local digitIdx - index into digitStack while emitting

    lineChar(",")
    absMagnitude := (numValue #> (0 - benchLog.LIM_BIG)) <# benchLog.LIM_BIG
    if absMagnitude < 0
        lineChar("-")
        absMagnitude := 0 - absMagnitude
    digitCount := 0
    repeat
        digitStack[digitCount] := absMagnitude // benchLog.DEC_RADIX
        absMagnitude /= benchLog.DEC_RADIX
        digitCount += 1
    until (absMagnitude == 0) or (digitCount == benchLog.MAX_DEC_DIGITS)
    repeat digitIdx from digitCount - 1 to 0
        lineChar("0" + digitStack[digitIdx])
        if (digitIdx > 0) and (digitIdx // benchLog.DIGITS_PER_GROUP == 0)
            lineChar("_")

PRI lineChar(charByte)
' Append one character to the RC-TEL line, dropping anything past the buffer (logger cog).
'
' @param charByte - the character to append

    if lgLineLen < LINE_BUF_BYTES - 1
        lgLine[lgLineLen] := charByte
        lgLineLen += 1
        lgLine[lgLineLen] := 0

CON { license }
